proposal.text:
{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "In executions requiring the demonstrated tool-push method, memory correctly retained method=tool_push and the initial execution scene. After the bar was grasped and raised, the policy repeatedly selected the opposite lateral staging position and a yaw reversed by approximately pi. After staging, it sometimes lifted again or moved to that opposite staging position instead of pushing. These are SUFFICIENCY failures in the action rules, not missing historical information.

Replace the signed tool-push geometry consistently throughout the instruction. Keep d=(T-C)/|T-C| and n=(-dy,dx), but define the staging TCP as C-0.10d-0.10n at z=0.02, closed, and the stroke endpoint as T-0.03d-0.10n at z=0.02, closed. The long free end must point along +n. In the raised-bar condition, identify the directed horizontal unit vector u from the grasp toward the long free end and command yaw wrap_to_pi(theta+atan2(ny,nx)-atan2(uy,ux)), where theta is the current TCP yaw. Remove the conflicting positive-n TCP offsets and negative-n free-end direction. Do not reduce this directed staging yaw modulo pi. Preserve the established staging orientation during the stroke and release; keep modulo-pi selection only for the initial grasp yaw.

Make the geometric branch priority explicit using these corrected positions. A closed hand holding the bar low near the corrected staging position, with motion frames showing transport and descent of an already-held bar, must execute the stroke rather than lift or restage. Accept approximately 0.015 m horizontal arrival error. The source lift applies only to a low held bar at its original grasp location after grasp descent, not to every low closed hand holding a bar. Recognizing staging arrival takes precedence over small discrepancies from fresh localization. At the reached stroke endpoint with the cube on the target, open using the existing nonidentical-waypoint release rule.

A separate failure occurred when the open hand had reached the overhead bar-grasp approach and correctly selected grasp descent, but reconstructed the grasp point more than 1 cm laterally from its intended location. The existing instruction to account for surface elevation is too vague for SUFFICIENCY. Specify that localization uses the physical end-face centers and longitudinal centerline, not an arbitrary silhouette corner or the measured undershooting TCP. Reconstruct the visible top surface using calibrated views: fit a common bar segment and surface elevation to corresponding endpoints and edges in the initial views and the current calibrated overhead wrist view, rather than projecting elevated surface pixels onto the table. Intersect rays with that fitted surface plane, take the midpoint across the bar width at each physical end, select the endpoint nearer the robot in world coordinates, and place G exactly 0.05 m inward along the reconstructed horizontal centerline. Cross-check the reconstructed segment by reprojection into both views before accepting G. Keep this same geometric definition for approach, descent, and lift; do not change the inward offset or substitute the reached TCP position. No episode-specific coordinates or unsupported fixed surface height should be added."
}

